## Implementing an Esolang
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

!!! tip "Tip"
    This demo builds on a few others: an awk [polyglot]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/demos/polyglots) for the lexer, the [`io.stack.*` structured stack]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/standard-lib) for state, and the idea that [everything is a target]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/language/#homoiconic) -- so a decoded program is just a stream of `make` goals.

[Underload](https://esolangs.org/wiki/Underload)[^1] is a small, stack-based, concatenative esoteric language: a program is a string of single-character commands operating on a stack of strings.  `(`...`)` pushes a literal, `~` swaps the top two, `:` duplicates, `!` discards, `*` concatenates, `a` wraps the top in parentheses, `S` prints it, and `^` pops the top and *runs it as Underload*.  That last one is what makes it Turing-complete -- and makes quines delightfully easy.

This demo implements a complete Underload interpreter with `compose.mk`, and the pieces map cleanly onto the standard library.  The **lexer** is an awk define that rewrites program text into one dispatchable goal per line; the **stack** is `io.stack.*`; each **operator** (`~`, `:`, `*`, ...) is its own `make` target; and the decoded program is run by `mk.kernel.each`, which dispatches each line as an instruction (order and repetition preserved).  The bundled programs include a quine, a palindromic quine, and an (abstracted) factorial.

```bash {.cli_example}
# Run the bundled demo programs:
$ ./demos/underload.mk

# Evaluate a program from stdin:
$ printf '(Hello, world!)S' | ./demos/underload.mk ul.eval

# A quine -- prints its own source:
$ printf '(a(:^)*S):^' | ./demos/underload.mk ul.eval
```

